The CLI is optional. [Obtain the development checkout](/docs/start-here/#get-the-source), then run its `bin/rivet` or add that directory to `PATH`. The launcher uses Lua by default; select LuaJIT with `RIVET_LUA=luajit rivet doctor`. Its filesystem and process adapter supports macOS and Linux.

## Shared creation options

```sh
rivet new ./engine --language cpp --standard 26 --modules false --preview
rivet new ./analysis_demo --language python --preview
rivet new ./compute_demo --language rust --kind workspace --preview
rivet new ./personal_tool --language swift --preview
rivet new ./interop --language c --standard 17 --preview
```

| Option | Values and behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `--language` | `cpp` (default), `c`, `python`, `rust`, or `swift`; additional providers can be registered in the running Lua process |
| `--name` | Project identifier; otherwise derived from the destination folder. Explicit names must satisfy the chosen provider's identifier rules. |
| `--kind` | `app` (default) or `library`; Rust also accepts `workspace` |
| `--license` | `none` (default), `MIT`, `Apache-2.0`, `BSD-3-Clause`, or `GPL-3.0-only`; only for `new` |
| `--license-holder` | Copyright holder; required when selecting a license |
| `--license-year` | Four-digit year; defaults to the current year |
| `--git` | Separate `true` or `false`; defaults to `false`. Only for `new`. |
| `--interactive` | Terminal questionnaire with a final creation confirmation |
| `--preview` | Print the complete plan without applying it |
| `--build` | Run the language's Build workflow after creation; only for `new` |
| `--preset` | Initial build choice, normally `debug` (default) or `release` |

Use `rivet new . --git true --preview` to create directly in the current folder. The folder supplies the suggested project name; `--name` overrides it. Automatic names normalize punctuation and spaces and prefix names that would be invalid or reserved. No nested folder is added.

Git initialization follows file writes and respects Git's configured default branch. An existing repository or worktree, including an enclosing repository, is reused. Rivet does not stage, commit, or add a remote. Missing Git fails an explicit initialization request before writes. If Git initialization itself fails afterward, project files remain and the error explains how to retry Git directly.

Each provider generates its own `.gitignore`, even when Git initialization is skipped. Existing rules are preserved after generated rules so user exceptions keep their priority. Other destination collisions are refused.

`--preview --build` only previews. A failed initial build leaves generated files available for correction. Python Build checks syntax; it does not configure an environment or produce a wheel. A new uv project therefore needs an explicit Configure before native tasks can use its environment.

## C++ creation options

These options apply to `--language cpp`. Choose **General C++** (`--profile general`) for the ordinary application/core starter.

| Option | Values and default |
| --- | --- |
| `--standard` | `17`, `20`, `23`, or `26` (default) |
| `--modules` | `true` or `false`; defaults to `false` in C++17 and `true` in later modes |
| `--module-layout` | `single` or `split` (default) |
| `--std-modules` | `true` or `false` (default); requires C++23+ and a compatible toolchain |
| `--profile` | `general` (default), `cpu`, `openmp`, `mpi`, `cuda`, `metal`, `webgpu`, `vulkan`, `opengl` |
| `--cuda-standard` | CUDA-only `17`, `20` (default), or `23`; independent of the host C++ mode |
| `--cuda-architectures` | CUDA-only `native` (default), `all`, `all-major`, or a quoted list such as `'80;90'`; numbers can have `-real` or `-virtual` suffixes |
| `--layout` | `feature` (default), `library`, or `flat` |
| `--style` | `LLVM` (default), `Google`, `Chromium`, `Mozilla`, `WebKit`, `Microsoft`, or `GNU` |
| `--header-guard` | `pragma` (default) or `ifndef` |
| `--generator` | `Ninja` (default) or quoted `"Unix Makefiles"`; Make requires both module options off |
| `--compiler` | Executable C++ compiler path, stored in local user presets |

Boolean options require separate, lowercase words: `--modules false`, not `--modules=false`, `--modules yes`, or a bare `--modules`. `--preview`, `--interactive`, and `--build` are switches without values. Use [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/) for module requirements and [HPC](/docs/hpc/) or [graphics](/docs/graphics/) for workload setup.

## Other language creation options

| Language | Options |
| --- | --- |
| C | `--standard 11`, `17` (default), or `23`; CMake/Ninja starter. Select its C compiler through local CMake presets. |
| Python | `--manager python` (default) or `uv`; generated package requires Python 3.11+ |
| Rust | `--edition 2021` or `2024` (default); `--kind app`, `library`, or `workspace` |
| Swift | `--kind app` or `library`; generated SwiftPM manifest requires Swift tools 6.0+ |

Provider-specific C++ flags such as formatting style, modules, or layout do not configure Python, Cargo, or SwiftPM. Use their native configuration files and the language guides.

## Add files and targets

Generated mutations require `.rivet.json`. Run inside that project or add `--root PATH`:

```sh
rivet file Renderer --kind class --target engine_core --preview
rivet target viewer --kind app --preview
rivet target renderer --kind library --link-to engine --preview
```

| Provider | `file` behavior | `target` behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++ | `source`, `header`, `pair`, `class`, `module`, `module_impl`, `c_source`, or `c_header` | Application or static library; optional `--link-to` |
| C | A named `.c` source in the chosen target | Application or static library |
| Python | A named module under the generated package | Add entry points directly in `pyproject.toml` |
| Rust | A named `.rs` file; declare it in the parent module yourself | Additional application under `src/bin/`; use Cargo for new libraries/workspace members |
| Swift | A source under a recorded target's conventional directory; preserves `Package.swift` | Application or library; refuses an externally edited generated package manifest |

For a C++ `module_impl`, provide `--module` with an existing module name. See [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) for path rules and [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/#native-tasks-and-targets) for customized packages.

## Dependencies and file lifecycle

The C++ dependency command registers a pinned CPM dependency; other providers use their native dependency tools:

```sh
rivet dependency fmt --repository fmtlib/fmt --tag 11.2.0 \
  --target fmt::fmt --link-to engine_core --preview
```

`--version` aliases `--tag`; use an exact revision, not a version range. Scaffolding does not fetch it; a later configure can. See [CPM dependencies](/docs/dependencies/).

`move FROM TO`, `remove PATH`, and `module-rename FROM TO` create plans for registered C/C++ files. `repair MANAGED_FILE` backs up an edited managed file and restores its recorded snapshot. These commands, plus `new`, `file`, `target`, and `dependency`, accept `--preview`. Omit it to apply the CLI plan. Preview is not a dry-run mode for build tasks. See [Existing projects and file changes](/docs/existing-projects/) and [recovery](/docs/troubleshooting/#recover-an-edited-managed-file).

## Project discovery and tasks

Commands search above the current directory for an Rivet manifest or a supported native project. CMake discovery skips ordinary nested source-list fragments in favor of a parent project declaration or presets. `--root PATH` selects a root explicitly. Existing CMake, Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM projects can run tasks without `.rivet.json`; they do not automatically gain generated-file ownership.

| Provider | Available tasks and sequencing |
| --- | --- |
| C/C++ | Configure; Build configures first; Test and Run configure and build first; Format configures first |
| Python | Configure environment explicitly; Check/Build checks syntax; Test, Run, Format, Lint, and Package use selected native tools |
| Rust | Configure reads metadata; Check, Build, Test, Run, Format, Lint, and Package invoke Cargo |
| Swift | Configure reads metadata; Check/Build invokes SwiftPM build; Test, Run, Format, Lint; Xcode handoff on macOS |

Workflows stop at the first failed step. No task silently installs optional formatters or linters. Native tools retain their ordinary dependency-resolution behavior: for example, Cargo builds may download declared dependencies, and an explicit uv Configure syncs its environment.

```sh
rivet build --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet test --root ./engine --preset release
rivet run --root ./engine --target viewer
rivet targets --root ./engine
```

For CMake, `--preset NAME` selects a configure preset. `--build-preset` and `--test-preset` select associated native presets; a unique association is selected automatically. `--build-target` overrides Build's target; Run builds the application selected with `--target`. A fresh existing project can be configured and run in one command. If it has several executable targets, choose one explicitly. [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) covers custom presets.

Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM use `debug` or `release` choices, not CMake preset files. Cargo binary selectors can use `package::binary`. Explicit runtime arguments, environments, working directories, and optional `benchmark`, `profile`, `submit`, `status`, and `cancel` hooks live in `.rivet.local.json`. See [Runtime options](/docs/task-options/).

## Inspect and configure

```sh
rivet doctor --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet cache --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet cache BUILD_TESTING OFF --root ./engine --preset debug
rivet styles
rivet layouts
rivet --help
```

Cache editing, styles, and layouts concern the CMake/C++ workflow. Doctor does not build or install tools and returns a nonzero status for reported errors; warnings alone do not fail it. Outside a project it reports available tools. Its [coverage table](/docs/troubleshooting/#what-doctor-checks) explains what a clean report can establish.

## Version and former Outset projects

`rivet --version` prints the plugin/CLI version. The launcher requires Lua; it is not a standalone compiled binary. Other tools also install a command named `rivet`; call this checkout's `bin/rivet` by path or through an explicit symlink rather than replacing another installed command.

Use `rivet migrate --root /path/to/project --preview` to review an Outset project's conversion, then repeat without `--preview` to apply it. Changed originals are backed up in `.rivet-recovery/`. The equivalent editor command is `:RivetMigrate`. Application names and module names stay the same; configure again to refresh the build and compilation database.

The `outset` launcher still delegates to Rivet for old shell scripts, prints a deprecation notice on stderr, and will be removed in 0.3.0; `OUTSET_LUA` is honored only through that launcher. Projects with an `.outset.json` manifest need migration before Rivet tasks and mutations run.
